(docs/BUGS_FOUND.md #162: a list or a map holding a float that is not equal
 to itself is unequal to every list or map, itself included. The manual has
 no nan literal, so one is made by subtracting an overflowed float from
 itself.)

a float called huge is 1000000000000.0.
Repeat 5 times, set huge to huge multiply huge.
a float called unordered is huge subtract huge.
If unordered is unordered then, Print "scalar: equal".
Otherwise, Print "scalar: not equal"..

a list called xs is [unordered].
a list called zs is [unordered].
If xs is xs then, Print "a list and itself: equal".
Otherwise, Print "a list and itself: not equal"..
If xs is zs then, Print "a list and a second list built the same way: equal".
Otherwise, Print "a list and a second list built the same way: not equal"..
If xs is not xs then, Print "a list is not itself".

a list called nested is [1, [unordered]].
If nested is nested then, Print "a nested list and itself: equal".
Otherwise, Print "a nested list and itself: not equal"..

a map called gauge is {"level": 1.5}.
Set gauge's "level" to unordered.
If gauge is gauge then, Print "a map and itself: equal".
Otherwise, Print "a map and itself: not equal"..

a list called plain is [1.5, "two"].
If plain is plain then, Print "a list of ordinary values and itself: equal".
a map called settled is {"level": 1.5}.
If settled is settled then, Print "a map of ordinary values and itself: equal".

To 'compare with itself' with a list called readings.
    If readings is readings then, Print "a list parameter and itself: equal".
    Otherwise, Print "a list parameter and itself: not equal"..

'compare with itself' of xs.
'compare with itself' of plain.
